trong hand on the Flop. The board presents a flush draw (two spades), but on a paired board like this, it is difficult for the opponent to have connected strongly unless they also hold a Queen or a pocket pair. Betting now risks folding out the opponent's weaker hands (air) from which you want to extract value. By checking, you disguise the strength of your hand, potentially inducing the opponent to bluff at the pot or allowing them to catch a card on the Turn that gives them a second-best hand.\n\nReason: To maximize value with a monster hand in a heads-up situation, checking is often superior to betting, as it keeps the opponent's range wide and invites aggressive actions from them.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
