 the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**River Decision: Check and Evaluate**\n\nAlright, here's the situation. It's hand 11 out of 20, I'm down a little, but still within striking distance with almost 20,000 chips. My pocket nines, 9 of Clubs and 9 of Hearts, looked promising pre-flop, but the board, K, J, 10, 8, 2, has completely changed things. I'm facing a serious straight-scare board.\n\nLet's break it down. I checked preflop and my opponent checked as well. The flop and turn were check-check as well. Now, it's the river and I have to act first. My nines give me a pair, but with that board, it's a weak pair at best. Any King, Jack, or Ten crushes me. Even a Queen could be problematic if my opponent has the right cards to make a straight. \n\nHolding the 9s does block some straights. A Q9 or 97 straight is unlikely.\n\nBut, given the history of check-check-check, my opponent is either very weak, potentially holding a very small pair or ace high, or they are just trying to control the pot.\n\nIf I bet, I might push a 10 or J off the pot, but I could also get snapped off by any of those cards I mentioned earlier or a Queen. Is the tiny pot worth the risk? If I check and opponent checks, I might win.\n\nThe best play, strategically, feels like checking. This gives me a chance to see a showdown at the lowest cost, which is the best outcome if I have the best hand. If I bet, I am likely getting called by better hands and losing. If I check and my opponent bets, I will fold and lose the pot. My nines beat the possibility of A-high or small pocket pairs, so there is potential. Given how the board is and the check-check behavior, a check is the best play here.\n\n**Final Answer: Action 0 (Check).**\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You hold a pair of 9s on a board of K-J-10-8-2. While you have a pair, there are three overcards on the board (K, J, 10), meaning any of those cards in the opponent's hand beats you. However, the game history shows the opponent checked on both the Flop and the Turn. This passivity suggests they likely do not have a strong King or Jack and might be on a draw or holding a weak hand like Ace-high or a small pair. Your pocket 9s serve as blockers to the straight draws (Q-9, 9-7), reducing the chance the opponent has a straight. Betting here turns your hand into a bluff that likely only folds out hands you are already beating (like A-high), while hands that beat you (like a weak 10 or J) would call. Checking allows you to get to a cheap showdown where you might win against a weak range or missed draws.\n\nReason: The opponent has shown no aggression, implying a weak hand. Checking maximizes the chance to reach showdown for free, potentially winning against Ace-high or lower pairs, while avoiding losing extra chips to a trapped King or Jack.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
