s are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Hand Analysis: River Decision**\n\nOkay, so I'm on hand 12 of 20, with 19,900 chips left. It looks like the blinds are 50/100, and I'm down 100 chips from the start, so I must have posted the big blind or called a pre-flop bet. The opponent, with around 20,100 chips, checked all the way down, which is the key here. The board is [10c, 5d, 4d, Jc, 8h], and I have [Ad, 3c]. I've got Ace-high, with a Jack as my kicker. No flush is possible. There's a straight available, but I don't have it.\n\nThe history shows the opponent checked pre-flop, flop, turn, and now the river. This tells me a lot. A pre-flop check implies a weak hand, maybe even a limped pot. The checked-down action suggests a passive player, either protecting a weak hand, trying to induce a bluff, or they just plain don't know what they're doing. The pot is small, which further leans towards a weaker holding, likely a missed draw or low pair. Betting here is risky because I'm only getting value from hands that are already worse than mine (K-high, Q-high) and losing to anything that connects with the board. They are most likely checking for value so as not to overcommit. Check back and see if my ace-high is good, since the pot size means I'm not risking too much. This is a clear checkdown situation.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: The hand has been checked down to the river on a board of [10c, 5d, 4d, Jc, 8h]. You hold [Ad, 3c], giving you an Ace-high hand. The opponent's passivity suggests a weak range, likely missed draws or low pairs. Your Ace-high has showdown value against their missed draws and lower high cards. Betting here is unlikely to be profitable because better hands (like any pair) will call, and worse hands (like King-high) will fold. Checking allows you to see the showdown for free, giving you the chance to win the pot if your Ace-high is the best hand